<h1>subgraph</h1>
<p>
(definition)
</p>

<p>
<strong>Definition:</strong>
A <a href="graph.html"><em>graph</em></a> whose <a href="vertex.html"><em>vertices</em></a> and <a href="edge.html"><em>edges</em></a> are <a href="subset.html"><em>subsets</em></a> of another <a href="graph.html"><em>graph</em></a>.
</p>

<p>
<strong>Formal Definition:</strong> A <a href="graph.html"><em>graph</em></a> G'=(V', E') is a subgraph of another graph  G=(V, E) iff  <ul> <li> V'&sube; V, and <li> E'&sube; E &and; ( (v<sub>1</sub>, v<sub>2</sub>)&isin; E'     &rarr; v<sub>1</sub>, v<sub>2</sub>&isin; V'). </ul>
</p>

<p>
<em>Note:
In general, a subgraph need not have all possible edges.  If a subgraph has every possible edge, it is an <a href="http://mathworld.wolfram.com/InducedSubgraph.html" target="_blank">induced subgraph</a>.</em>
</p>

<p>Authors: <a href="../Other/contrib.html#authorPEB">PEB</a>,<a href="../Other/contrib.html#authorAL">AL</a></p>

